  "finalReply": "Created polyglot config parser at `/app/config/parser.sh.py` (executable).\n\n**Polyglot technique:** `#!/bin/bash` + `\"\"\":\"` line is a bash `:` no-op and a Python triple-quoted string opener. Bash code runs then `exit`s; Python treats bash as a string closed by `# \"\"\"` and runs its own identical logic. Verified `bash parser.sh.py`, `python3 parser.sh.py`, and direct `./parser.sh.py` all work with no warnings.\n\n**Behavior (identical in both runtimes):**\n- Validates 2 non-empty args, else `stderr` + exit 1 (including whitespace-only key).\n- Validates exists + regular file, readable via permission bits (`stat %A` / `mode & 0o444` so `chmod 000` fails even as root), non-empty via `-s` + `grep [^space]`, else `stderr` + exit 1.\n- Parses `key=value` and `key: value` (trims whitespace, skips blanks/`#`/`;` comments, splits on earliest `=`/`:` so URLs like `postgresql://...` work, preserves `=`/`:` in values, last duplicate wins, empty values allowed).\n- Malformed (no separator, empty key, key with whitespace/`=`/`:`) → `stderr` + exit 1 after validating whole file.\n- Found → value on `stdout` exit 0; valid but missing → `KEY_NOT_FOUND` exit 0.\n\n**Verified:** `PORT→8080`, `DATABASE_URL`, `EMPTY_VALUE`, `KEY_WITH_SPACES`, `KEY_NOT_FOUND`, missing/empty/blank/unreadable files, invalid/empty-key/bad-key formats, missing/empty keys, `:`/`=` with spacing, duplicates, earliest-separator, late-malformed, and direct exec — bash/python outputs and exit codes match.",
